{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKp2heY65P4AonQFuuE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SesNrtdhTSR8aLBhtiMSBk","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nimport re\nt=open('paper.tex').read()\ncites=re.findall(r'\\\\cite[a-z]*\\{([^}]*)\\}',t)\nfrom collections import Counter\nc=Counter(k.strip() for x in cites for k in x.split(','))\nkeys=re.findall(r'@\\w+\\{([^,]+),',open('references.bib').read())\nfor k in keys: print(k,c.get(k,0))\nE\ngrep -n \"Related Work\" -A12 paper.tex | head -60; grep -n \"y_{qk}\" -B6 -A6 paper.tex | head -50; grep -n \"biased estimate of that\" -B4 -A4 paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2509,"cache_read_input_tokens":54100,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2509},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a3b1cc68-ceca-4955-bf61-426285eb4864","uuid":"83c3fdab-46ad-480c-97cb-32f66ebefb95","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:48:49.148Z","request_id":"req_011CgwKp2BA1oYmzEyVpbd2U"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SesNrtdhTSR8aLBhtiMSBk","type":"tool_result","content":"Dwork2014PreservingSV 1\nDwork2015TheRH 1\nDwork2015GeneralizationIA 1\nFeldman2017CalibratingNT 1\nDwork2017GuiltfreeDR 1\nMarchant2024AdaptiveDA 1\nUllman2017TechnicalPB 1\nWang2024MMLUPro 1\nKhattab2023DSPy 2\nOpsahlOng2024MIPRO 2\nPolo2024EfficientMultiPrompt 1\nShi2024PromptBAI 2\nSclar2023Quantifying 1\nBlum2015Ladder 1\nChernozhukov2013Gaussian 1\nJamieson2016SuccessiveHalving 1\nRecht2019ImageNet 1\nQwen2024Qwen25 1\nBerk2013ValidPSI 1\nGneiting2007Scoring 2\nAndrews2024InferenceOnWinners 1\nHardt2014Hard 1\nMiller2024ErrorBars 1\nEfron2011Tweedie 1\nEfron1973Stein 1\nWilson1927Probable 1\nEfron1997Bootstrap632 1\n78:\\section{Related Work}\n79-\\textbf{Adaptive data analysis.} Reusing a holdout set across adaptively chosen analyses invalidates standard inference. \\citet{Dwork2014PreservingSV,Dwork2015GeneralizationIA} showed that differential-privacy-style noise preserves validity, \\citet{Dwork2015TheRH} gave the reusable holdout, \\citet{Feldman2017CalibratingNT} calibrated the noise to variance, and \\citet{Marchant2024AdaptiveDA} extended the setting to growing data; \\citet{Hardt2014Hard} showed that preventing false discovery in fully interactive analysis is computationally hard, and \\citet{Ullman2017TechnicalPB,Dwork2017GuiltfreeDR} give overviews. The Ladder \\citep{Blum2015Ladder} reports leaderboard scores of repeated submissions through a thresholded mechanism. These methods change \\emph{what is released} to the analyst. Our reporter instead leaves the search untouched and corrects the report afterwards from the log; it does not provide their worst-case guarantees, and we do not study adaptive reporting across several released scores.\n80-\n81-\\textbf{Inference after selection.} Post-selection inference constructs valid intervals for selected targets \\citep{Berk2013ValidPSI}; \\citet{Andrews2024InferenceOnWinners} study inference on the best-performing unit, the direct statistical analogue of a best-of-search score; and \\citet{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian} give Gaussian approximations for maxima that underlie bootstrap critical values. Empirical-Bayes shrinkage \\citep{Efron1973Stein} and its connection to selection bias \\citep{Efron2011Tweedie} motivate our use of a hierarchical model. The bootstrap optimism correction \\citep{Efron1997Bootstrap632} motivates the question-resampling baseline. Benchmark overfitting under repeated reuse has been probed on image classification by collecting a fresh test set \\citep{Recht2019ImageNet}.\n82-\n83-\\textbf{Prompt search and LLM evaluation.} DSPy \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy} and MIPRO \\citep{OpsahlOng2024MIPRO} automate prompt and pipeline optimization, best-arm formulations bound evaluation cost \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}, successive halving \\citep{Jamieson2016SuccessiveHalving} is a standard budget allocator, performance is sensitive to prompt formatting \\citep{Sclar2023Quantifying}, multi-prompt evaluation can be made sample-efficient \\citep{Polo2024EfficientMultiPrompt}, and error bars on benchmark scores have been advocated \\citep{Miller2024ErrorBars}. None of these works supplies a post-search score with selection-aware uncertainty. We use the Wilson interval \\citep{Wilson1927Probable} for the baselines.\n84-\n85-\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n86-\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\n87-A system is searched over $K$ configurations on $N$ benchmark questions. A searcher produces a log $\\mathcal L=\\{(q_i,k_i,y_i)\\}$ of question indices, configuration indices and binary outcomes, and selects $\\hat k$ as a function of $\\mathcal L$ only. The target is the selected configuration's accuracy $\\theta_{\\hat k}$ on fresh questions, which the evaluator knows. A reporter receives $\\mathcal L$, $\\hat k$, $N$, $K$, the searcher tag and an anonymous subject code per question, and returns $(\\hat\\theta,\\ell,u)$ with $0\\le \\ell\\le\\hat\\theta\\le u\\le 1$. We score the interval with the interval score at level $1-\\alpha=0.90$,\n88-\\begin{equation}\n89-\\IS_\\alpha(\\ell,u;\\theta)=(u-\\ell)+\\tfrac{2}{\\alpha}\\,(\\ell-\\theta)_+ + \\tfrac{2}{\\alpha}\\,(\\theta-u)_+ ,\n90-\\end{equation}\n95-\n96-\\subsection{EB-IRT reporter}\n97-% @evidence[[analysis/kappa_values.json]]\n98-% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/reporter.py]]\n99-\\textbf{Model.} With $\\sigma$ the logistic function, each logged outcome follows\n100-\\begin{equation}\n101:y_{qk}\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}\\big(\\sigma(a_k-b_q)\\big),\\quad a_k\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tau^2),\\quad b_q\\sim\\mathcal N(m_{s(q)},s^2),\n102-\\end{equation}\n103-where $a_k$ is a configuration effect, $b_q$ a question difficulty and $m_{s}$ the mean difficulty of subject $s$ (the question's subject code; synthetic worlds have a single code). Subject means are shrunk toward the global mean with five pseudo-questions. The fresh-question accuracy of configuration $k$ is $\\theta_k=\\mathbb E_b[\\sigma(a_k-b)]$, where $b$ is drawn from the mixture of subjects weighted by the observed subject frequencies.\n104-\n105-\\textbf{Empirical-Bayes fit.} Using only the sparse triples, we alternate damped Newton sweeps for $(a,b)$ with variance updates $\\tau^2\\leftarrow\\frac1K\\big(\\sum_k\\hat a_k^2+\\sum_k S_k^{-1}\\big)$ and $s^2\\leftarrow\\frac1N\\big(\\sum_q(\\hat b_q-m_{s(q)})^2+\\sum_q H_q^{-1}\\big)$, where $S_k$ is the Schur-complement posterior precision of $a_k$ after integrating out difficulties and $H_q$ the diagonal Hessian for $b_q$. These Laplace corrections stop $\\tau^2$ from collapsing or inflating. Fitting uses bincount-based vector operations, so exhaustive and halving logs share one code path; the fit stops after at most 30 sweeps or a 1.5\\,s wall-clock budget.\n106-\n107-\\textbf{Ignorability.} Exhaustive search and successive halving choose which entries to observe and which configuration to select using only previously observed outcomes. The likelihood of the log is therefore unaffected by the design, and a likelihood-based posterior is valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction, provided the model is adequately specified. This is a motivation, not a proof for the real-outcome worlds, whose subject-by-configuration structure a one-dimensional difficulty model only approximates.\n55-% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.P4.coverage]]\n56-% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.P6.coverage]]\n57-\n58-\\section{Introduction}\n59:Prompt and pipeline optimizers such as DSPy and MIPRO search over instructions, demonstrations and multi-call structures on a benchmark sample \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy,OpsahlOng2024MIPRO}, and in practice the best configuration is often reported with the score it attained on the very sample used to select it. Configuration search is also cast as best-arm identification under a fixed evaluation budget \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}. The reported number is the maximum, over many candidates, of noisy estimates, and the candidate that attains it was chosen \\emph{because} its estimate was high. It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection. Whether comparisons and decisions built on such numbers are trustworthy depends on how large the gap is and on what controls it.\n60-\n61-We ask two questions. \\textbf{(Q1)} For a system whose configuration is chosen on a finite question set, how far is the reported score from its accuracy on fresh questions, what governs the gap, and does it threaten conclusions drawn from reported scores? \\textbf{(Q2)} Can a better reporting rule be designed, and how should its reliability be tested?\n62-\n63-Our answer to Q2 is a post-hoc \\emph{reporter} that never touches the search: it receives only the evaluation log of (question, configuration, outcome) triples, the selected configuration and basic