in fully interactive analysis is computationally hard, and \\citet{Ullman2017TechnicalPB,Dwork2017GuiltfreeDR} give overviews. The Ladder \\citep{Blum2015Ladder} reports leaderboard scores of repeated submissions through a thresholded mechanism. These methods change \\emph{what is released} to the analyst. Our reporter instead leaves the search untouched and corrects the report afterwards from the log; it does not provide their worst-case guarantees, and we do not study adaptive reporting across several released scores.\n80-\n81-\\textbf{Inference after selection.} Post-selection inference constructs valid intervals for selected targets \\citep{Berk2013ValidPSI}; \\citet{Andrews2024InferenceOnWinners} study inference on the best-performing unit, the direct statistical analogue of a best-of-search score; and \\citet{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian} give Gaussian approximations for maxima that underlie bootstrap critical values. Empirical-Bayes shrinkage \\citep{Efron1973Stein} and its connection to selection bias \\citep{Efron2011Tweedie} motivate our use of a hierarchical model. The bootstrap optimism correction \\citep{Efron1997Bootstrap632} motivates the question-resampling baseline. Benchmark overfitting under repeated reuse has been probed on image classification by collecting a fresh test set \\citep{Recht2019ImageNet}.\n82-\n83-\\textbf{Prompt search and LLM evaluation.} DSPy \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy} and MIPRO \\citep{OpsahlOng2024MIPRO} automate prompt and pipeline optimization, best-arm formulations bound evaluation cost \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}, successive halving \\citep{Jamieson2016SuccessiveHalving} is a standard budget allocator, performance is sensitive to prompt formatting \\citep{Sclar2023Quantifying}, multi-prompt evaluation can be made sample-efficient \\citep{Polo2024EfficientMultiPrompt}, and error bars on benchmark scores have been advocated \\citep{Miller2024ErrorBars}. None of these works supplies a post-search score with selection-aware uncertainty