{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKusZtgn49CXHM7Tb36","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I added the missing citations to the three flagged places in `paper.tex`. The paper compiles with no undefined citations, and the evidence verifier reports zero dangling references. I couldn't run the figure-manifest check (it can't find the `src` module here), and `compile_latex.sh` needs `pdftotext`, which isn't installed, so I compiled with `pdflatex` and `bibtex` directly.\n\nThe earlier retrieval run had added no new references, so I looked up six directly by identifier or title (arXiv, Crossref, OpenAlex). I read the title, authors and year of each in the metadata returned by those sources. For the Cawley and Talbot entry, OpenAlex returned no venue, so the JMLR volume and pages were filled in from memory.\n\n- **IRT background (method):** the model equation is now described as a Rasch-type item response model. It cites Bock and Aitkin (1981), who estimate item parameters by marginal maximum likelihood with EM. The text contrasts this with the Newton and Laplace-corrected fit used here. The related work also cites Martínez-Plumed et al. (2019) on item response theory applied to machine-learning classifiers.\n- **Selection bias (introduction and related work):** I added Cawley and Talbot (2010) on over-fitting in model selection and Feldman et al. (2019) on test-set reuse. The related work now says explicitly that this paper measures the same optimism for searched LLM configurations, with ground truth from the evaluator.\n- **Prompt optimizers:** I added Zhou et al. (APE) and Yang et al. (OPRO) to the introduction and the prompt-search paragraph.\n\nThe 30-reference cap forced me to drop three entries: Ullman 2017, Dwork 2017 (the Dwork overview) and Marchant 2024. I removed their sentences in the adaptive-data-analysis paragraph and updated `claims_grounding.json` to match.\n\nI left the Roelofs me